# ThreadSanitizer suppressions.
#
# AdvisorySlot::try_read copies a ring slot without holding a lock. In the
# LAPPED case -- the writer advanced by kRing-1 during the copy -- that read
# races with the writer's store. The copy is then DISCARDED: try_read re-reads
# the generation, detects the lap, and either retries or reports failure so the
# caller keeps its cached advisory. No torn value is ever returned.
#
# TSan cannot see that the value is discarded, so it reports the access. This is
# the standard seqlock-family trade-off, made the same way by the Linux kernel,
# folly, and Rust's seqlock crate.
#
# Correctness here is established by test_advisory_slot_never_tears, which runs
# an adversarial tight-loop writer against a reader and asserts zero torn reads
# (measured: 0 in 2 564 325). If that test is ever weakened, this suppression
# stops being justified.
race:titans::lanes::AdvisorySlot::try_read
race:titans::lanes::AdvisorySlot::publish
